==Aftermath==
[[File:Argentina celebrando copa.jpg|thumb|[[Diego Maradona]] (with the World Cup) and teammates celebrating the title]]
The second World Cup won by Argentina is regarded by many as the most important victory for an Argentine side.<ref>[http://www.laizquierdadiario.com/Mundial-%C2%B486-a-30-anos-de-la-mayor-hazana-del-futbol-argentino Mundial '86: a 30 años de la mayor hazaña del fútbol argentino] by Augusto Dorado, 29 Jun 2016 {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160906033157/http://www.laizquierdadiario.com/Mundial-%C2%B486-a-30-anos-de-la-mayor-hazana-del-futbol-argentino|date=6 September 2016}}</ref> Four years later, both teams met in the [[1990 FIFA World Cup final|final]] of the [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990 World Cup]], with West Germany winning. This marked the first time two World Cup finalists met twice, a record later shared with [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] and [[Italy national football team|Italy]], the [[1970 FIFA World Cup|1970]] and [[1994 FIFA World Cup|1994]] World Cup finalists. Argentina and Germany met in the [[2014 FIFA World Cup final|2014 final]] for a record third time as finalists.

With the 1986 defeat, German manager Franz Beckenbauer gained the distinction of having lost a World Cup final as a player (in 1966) and a manager. In 1990 he managed Germany to victory, becoming a winner of the World Cup as player (in 1974) and as manager.

{{cquote|The games are won by the players on the field, but behind it there must be a machinery that works. There I include players, coaching staff and leaders. If you all pull in the same direction, you can think of titles. That's what happened in Mexico '86.<ref>[https://www.elgrafico.com.ar/articulo/1088/1009/argentina-86 Argentina 86] on ''El Gráfico'' {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190328030133/https://www.elgrafico.com.ar/articulo/1088/1009/argentina-86|date=28 March 2019}}</ref>|author= [[Carlos Bilardo]], manager of Argentina}}

Germany won their fourth title in 2014. Argentina would not see another world title for 36 years until their win over [[France national football team|France]] in the [[2022 FIFA World Cup final|2022 final]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2022/dec/18/argentina-france-world-cup-2022-final-live|title=2022 World Cup final: Argentina 3-3 France (aet, 4-2 on pens) – as it happened|date=December 18, 2022|website=the Guardian|access-date=11 May 2024|archive-date=4 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230204082148/https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2022/dec/18/argentina-france-world-cup-2022-final-live|url-status=live}}</ref>

The pitch of the Azteca stadium would not see another World Cup match until the [[2026 FIFA World Cup|2026]] opening between co-host [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]] and [[South Africa national football team|South Africa]].

